      "Name": "body",
      "Html": "\u003Cstyle class=\u0022code-analysis-syntax\u0022\u003E\n.docs-content pre{overflow-x:auto;margin:1rem 0 1.75rem;padding:1.25rem 1.4rem;border:1px solid rgba(20,42,72,.16);border-left:4px solid #0e6fae;border-radius:4px;background:#f7fbfe;color:#10263a}\n.docs-content pre code{white-space:pre;color:inherit;background:transparent;font-size:1rem;line-height:1.65}\n.language-csharp .syntax-comment{color:#587287;font-style:italic}.language-csharp .syntax-string{color:#8a3d12}.language-csharp .syntax-keyword{color:#6542a6;font-weight:700}.language-csharp .syntax-number{color:#096b65}.language-csharp .syntax-type{color:#0a6680;font-weight:600}\n\u003C/style\u003E\u003Cheader class=\u0022docs-page-header\u0022\u003E\u003Ch1\u003E[page[title]]\u003C/h1\u003E\u003Cp\u003EModels must not declare methods.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/header\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EWhat the Standard says\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u0026ldquo;Data Carrier Models\u0026rdquo; \u0026mdash; Data-carrier models exist to transport data points between parts of a system. Keeping behavior out of those models preserves that single purpose and leaves business operations in services. \u003Ca href=\u0022https://github.com/hassanhabib/The-Standard/blob/master/0.%20Introduction/0.1%20Purposing,%20Modeling%20\u0026%20Simulation.md#01200-data-carrier-models\u0022 target=\u0022_blank\u0022 rel=\u0022noreferrer\u0022\u003Eread it here\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EWhy it matters\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EModels are data carriers, so methods give them a second architectural nature and hide behaviour inside transported state. Keeping models behaviour-free makes serialization predictable and ensures business rules remain in services where they can be protected and tested.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EBad code example\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cpre\u003E\u003Ccode class=\u0022language-csharp\u0022\u003E\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-comment\u0022\u003E// Violates STXM001: Models must not declare methods.\u003C/span\u003E\n\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-keyword\u0022\u003Eclass\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EStudent\u003C/span\u003E\n{\n    \u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EValueTask\u003C/span\u003E\u0026lt;\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-keyword\u0022\u003Estring\u003C/span\u003E\u0026gt; \u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EGetStatus\u003C/span\u003E() =\u0026gt; \u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EValueTask\u003C/span\u003E.\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EFromResult\u003C/span\u003E(\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EStatus\u003C/span\u003E);\n    \u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-keyword\u0022\u003Estring\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EStatus\u003C/span\u003E { get; set; }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EGood code example\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cpre\u003E\u003Ccode class=\u0022language-csharp\u0022\u003E\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-comment\u0022\u003E// Follows STXM001\u003C/span\u003E\n\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-keyword\u0022\u003Eclass\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EStudent\u003C/span\u003E\n{\n    \u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-keyword\u0022\u003Estring\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EStatus\u003C/span\u003E { get; set; }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E"
    }
